# Trivy ignore list — keep entries minimal and justified.
# Format: one advisory ID per line. Optional `exp: YYYY-MM-DD` for a hard expiry.
# See: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/#trivyignore

# --- Unfixable transitive findings via matrix-bot-sdk@0.8.0 ---
# The deprecated `request` library (and its companions request-promise,
# request-promise-core, tough-cookie, form-data, qs, uuid) is pulled in by
# matrix-bot-sdk and has no upstream patch. Tracked in SECURITY.md.
# Re-evaluate when matrix-bot-sdk migrates off `request` or when we
# switch SDKs.
#
# CVE-2023-28155 — request: SSRF via cross-protocol redirect.
# CVE-2026-41907 — uuid 3.4.0: missing buffer bounds check in v3/v5 when a
#   `buf` argument is supplied. `request` calls uuid() with no arguments, so
#   the vulnerable code path is unreachable from our usage. uuid is fixed in
#   v11+; request@2.88.2 hard-pins uuid@^3.3.2, so there is no patch path
#   short of replacing matrix-bot-sdk.
CVE-2023-28155
CVE-2026-41907

# The six distroless OpenSSL entries that used to live here (CVE-2026-31789
# CRITICAL, plus CVE-2026-28387/28388/28389/28390/45447 HIGH) were removed on
# 2026-07-29 by moving the runtime base from debian12 to debian13. Bookworm
# ships libssl3 3.0.18-1~deb12u2 with no fixed package for that suite; trixie
# reports 0 HIGH/CRITICAL. The findings are fixed, not suppressed.
#
# Note: runtime images are distroless (no npm, no shell), so CVEs in npm's own
# vendored deps (brace-expansion, undici, …) never ship and need no entries.
